WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.920 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A lunchbox feels heavy, while a bottle looks full.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:05.400 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Are we measuring the same attribute?

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.400 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine packing for a picnic.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.360 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We need a balance for mass and a marked container for
capacity.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.135 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Let us choose useful units before measuring.

00:00:14.135 --> 00:00:19.176 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to measure mass and capacity using
scales and combined units.

00:00:19.176 --> 00:00:20.736 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:20.736 --> 00:00:23.336 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can distinguish mass from capacity.

00:00:23.336 --> 00:00:25.776 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can read equal scale intervals.

00:00:25.776 --> 00:00:29.863 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can combine kilograms and grams or litres and millilitres.

00:00:37.663 --> 00:00:38.023 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Mass describes how heavy an object is; capacity describes
how much a container can hold.

00:00:38.023 --> 00:00:40.583 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use grams or kilograms for mass, and millilitres or litres
for capacity.

00:00:40.583 --> 00:00:41.903 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One kilogram is one thousand grams.

00:00:41.903 --> 00:00:46.223 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One litre is one thousand millilitres.

00:00:46.223 --> 00:00:51.489 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
On every scale, count equal intervals before deciding what
one interval represents.

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:55.689 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A bag has a mass of one kilogram and three hundred and fifty
grams.

00:00:55.689 --> 00:01:01.009 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Picture one thousand grams as a whole kilogram, with the
smaller amount added alongside.

00:01:01.009 --> 00:01:04.314 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What single gram reading would describe the same mass?

00:01:04.314 --> 00:01:09.674 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One thousand plus three hundred and fifty is one thousand
three hundred and fifty grams.

00:01:09.674 --> 00:01:13.274 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The bag has not become heavier because we changed its name.

00:01:13.274 --> 00:01:16.968 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Kilograms and grams are two units for the same attribute.

00:01:16.968 --> 00:01:20.968 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A jug holds two litres and two hundred and fifty
millilitres.

00:01:20.968 --> 00:01:23.048 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Convert the whole litres first.

00:01:23.048 --> 00:01:27.168 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two groups of one thousand millilitres make two thousand
millilitres.

00:01:27.168 --> 00:01:29.718 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then include the extra marked amount.

00:01:29.718 --> 00:01:33.758 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The capacity is two thousand two hundred and fifty
millilitres.

00:01:33.758 --> 00:01:40.798 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
On a scale from zero to one thousand with four equal
intervals, each interval would be two hundred and fifty
millilitres.

00:01:40.798 --> 00:01:43.315 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Count intervals, not just tick marks.

00:01:43.315 --> 00:01:46.955 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Grams and millilitres are not interchangeable labels.

00:01:46.955 --> 00:01:50.755 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A balance measures mass; a marked jug measures capacity.

00:01:50.755 --> 00:01:54.755 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Equal numerical readings do not make those attributes the
same.

00:01:54.755 --> 00:01:57.711 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Read the instrument and include the correct unit.

00:01:57.711 --> 00:02:02.231 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A balance points halfway between five hundred and six
hundred grams.

00:02:02.231 --> 00:02:03.911 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What mass does it show?

00:02:03.911 --> 00:02:07.010 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Look at the model, and keep your prediction in mind.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:11.890 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Halfway is fifty grams beyond five hundred, giving five
hundred and fifty grams.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:16.423 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The scale is measuring mass, so the answer needs grams, not
millilitres.

00:02:16.423 --> 00:02:19.583 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose the attribute, unit and instrument together.

00:02:19.583 --> 00:02:24.103 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For a practical challenge, use a kitchen balance or marked
jug with an adult.

00:02:24.103 --> 00:02:29.743 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict a reading, measure carefully, and explain the
difference between your estimate and observation.

00:02:29.743 --> 00:02:32.263 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reflect on which model helped you and why.
